module bindings.ruby.dragonflyconvertsteprawarguments;

// ImageMagick's convert command parses a leading dash as an option, so a value
// spelled `-write /var/www/shell.rb` is one shell-safe token that convert reads
// as an option, not a filename. Dragonfly's ImageMagick plugin exposes exactly
// that surface: `Content#process!` / `Content#generate!` dispatch to a
// registered job step by name, and the plugin's `:convert` step is the raw one
// -- its argument string is interpolated into the convert command line verbatim
// (`"#{convert_command} ... #{args} #{new_path}"`), after per-word shell
// escaping that stops metacharacters and nothing else. Job steps are reachable
// from the URL-driven job API, so the argument is request data whenever the
// application runs the server without URL signatures.
//
// The remediated plugin keeps the command assembly but stops dispatching the
// raw step: every remaining call site validates its parameters first
// (ParamValidators, an option whitelist, or a geometry regex) and calls the
// Commands module directly, so no value the URL controls reaches the convert
// argument list unvalidated.
//
// The call itself carries the discrimination, so the binding is a callExpr:
// `callee.method in [...]` compares the final segment only, and the argument
// needle reads the step symbol. Residual, stated rather than hidden: the
// needle is a substring over the argument literals, so a fixed string
// mentioning "convert" would match too -- across every .rb file in the corpus
// clones these two method names occur only in this plugin, so the shape has no
// other caller to mislabel.

binding dragonflyConvertStepRawArguments {
  query pattern callExpr where callee.method in ["process!", "generate!"] and args.any.literal contains "convert"
  emit sink code.CommandOptionInjectionSink at args[1]
  emit issue code.CommandOptionInjection at call
}
